# 13. Why Changing the Proxy Does Not Always Fix a CAPTCHA

Suppose a session receives repeated CAPTCHAs.

Changing the IP may change one variable.

But the session may still have:

* The same browser profile
* The same cookies
* The same account
* The same behavior
* The same request rate
* The same automation workflow

Therefore:

```text id="k1xwz8"
Change IP
     ↓
Only Network Layer Changes
     ↓
Other Risk Signals Remain
```

This explains why proxy switching should not be treated as a universal CAPTCHA solution.

---

# 14. The Browser Profile Can Matter More Than Expected

A browser profile can retain information across sessions.

Depending on the browser and website, this can include:

* Cookies
* Local storage
* Session data
* Browser configuration
* Extensions
* Login information

If the same profile is repeatedly used across substantially different network environments, the overall session history can become more complicated.

Profile isolation can help make browser environments easier to organize.

See [Browser Profile Isolation](../docs/browser-profile-isolation.md).

