# 15. Proxy Stability Is Often More Useful Than Constant Rotation

For many account-based workflows, stability is valuable.

A simple architecture may look like:

```text id="7r5n1v"
Account A
   ↓
Profile A
   ↓
Network A

Account B
   ↓
Profile B
   ↓
Network B
```

This creates a straightforward mapping.

Instead of:

```text id="gq4n4w"
Account A
   ↓
IP A → IP B → IP C → IP D
```

a stable configuration can be easier to monitor, troubleshoot, and maintain.

The correct strategy depends on the use case and the website's requirements.

---

# 16. Proxy Latency Can Affect Automation Reliability

Proxy quality is not only about reputation.

Connection performance matters too.

High latency or unstable connections can cause:

* Page timeouts
* Failed requests
* Incomplete page loads
* Automation errors
* Repeated retries

Repeated failures can make an automated workflow behave differently from a normal browsing session.

For this reason, proxy evaluation should include technical reliability as well as reputation.

---

# 17. Proxy Failures Can Create Unintended Patterns

Imagine an automation workflow configured to retry whenever a proxy request fails.

A poor connection could create:

```text id="z4j0x1"
Request
   ↓
Timeout
   ↓
Retry
   ↓
Timeout
   ↓
Retry
   ↓
New Request
```

A large number of retries may generate unnecessary traffic.

This is why robust automation should include:

* Reasonable retry limits
* Timeouts
* Error handling
* Logging
* Proxy health checks

See [Browser Automation Best Practices](../automation/automation-best-practices.md).

